{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2350" -> designer/boards/RP2350/RP2350.json -> const RP2350Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2350",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "Pimoroni Tiny 2350" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico 2 (RP2350)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040/RP2350 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // No genuinely bidirectional-but-input-only pins confirmed on this
  // board beyond the chip-level default.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. This is the plain (non-WiFi)
  // RP2350A-based Tiny 2350 — no onboard radio, unlike the separate
  // Pimoroni Pico Plus 2 W / Plasma 2350 W boards which live in the
  // "rp2040w" chip bucket instead.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  // RP2040/RP2350 boot from an internal ROM/BOOTSEL, not GPIO-driven
  // flash-mode strapping pins like ESP32/ESP8266 — no boot-time
  // restrictions to flag at the chip level.
  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040/RP2350 family audit, button/LED/NeoPixel
  // tagging, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: "Pimoroni Tiny 2350" (PIM721, RP2350A, 4MB flash). This
  // board's own pins_arduino.h declares PIN_LED (19u) with the comment
  // "RGB LED on pins 18-20. Use the Green LED element on pin 19 as
  // LED_BUILTIN" — i.e. PIN_LED only names ONE of three channels of a
  // single onboard RGB LED, not a plain single-color LED — identical
  // wording/pin-numbering to its RP2040 sibling
  // variants/rp2040/rp2040/pimoroni_tiny2040/pins_arduino.h. No
  // PIN_BUTTON alias is declared anywhere in this board's
  // pins_arduino.h.
  //
  // Sources consulted (WebSearch/WebFetch, 2026-07-13):
  //  1. shop.pimoroni.com/en-us/products/tiny-2350 (via pishop.us mirror,
  //     fetched directly — shop.pimoroni.com itself returned HTTP 503 at
  //     fetch time, so the pishop.us reseller page — which republishes
  //     Pimoroni's own product copy verbatim — was used instead):
  //     "The RGB LED is connected to GP18-GP20 and active low (so the
  //     on/off state will work in the opposite way to the LED on a
  //     Raspberry Pi Pico). You can PWM the pins to dim the LED." and,
  //     for the button: "GPIO Pin: GP23", "Logic: active low", "you can
  //     use the BOOT button as a user switch". Also lists a Qw/ST
  //     (I2C) connector and a separate reset button as other onboard
  //     features.
  //  2. forums.pimoroni.com/t/tiny-2350-rgb-led-pwm-pico-sdk/25465
  //     (WebFetch): sample code `TINY2350_LED_R_PIN = 18`,
  //     `TINY2350_LED_G_PIN = 19`, `TINY2350_LED_B_PIN = 20` —
  //     independently confirms the exact channel-to-GPIO mapping and
  //     restates "The RGB LED is connected to GP18-GP20 and active low
  //     ... so the on/off state will work in the opposite way to the LED
  //     on a Raspberry Pi Pico" (i.e. common-anode/active-low, same
  //     polarity as the Tiny 2040).
  //  3. WebSearch aggregating shop.pimoroni.com/PiShop.us/PiShop.ca/Pi
  //     Hut/Kiwi Electronics listings (2026-07-13): consistently repeats
  //     "the BOOT button... some clever circuitry that lets you use the
  //     boot button as a user controllable switch... wired to GP23 and
  //     active low" — the identical "clever circuitry" phrasing already
  //     confirmed on the Tiny 2040's product page to describe its
  //     diode-isolated BOOT/DFU-select circuit (see
  //     variants/rp2040/rp2040/pimoroni_tiny2040/board.json and its
  //     notes.txt), indicating the same protection mechanism is reused
  //     on this board's GP23. No source found that spells out the word
  //     "diode" for this specific model, but the button's GPIO number
  //     (23), electrical behaviour (active-low, safe to read as a user
  //     switch), and the verbatim recurring "clever circuitry" marketing
  //     phrase all match the Tiny 2040 precedent exactly, and Tiny 2350
  //     is marketed by Pimoroni as the direct RP2350 drop-in successor
  //     to the Tiny 2040 in the same tiny form factor.
  //
  // Per-pin findings:
  //   GPIO18 — RGB LED red channel. Tagged led_r_low (active-low,
  //     confirmed by sources #1 and #2 above). pwm_output kept — vendor
  //     docs explicitly describe PWM-dimming these pins.
  //   GPIO19 (PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN) — RGB LED green channel, the one
  //     channel this board's pins_arduino.h names as LED_BUILTIN. Tagged
  //     led_g_low for the same reasons as GPIO18. This is NOT a plain
  //     single-color LED_BUILTIN despite the alias name — it only drives
  //     one third of the physical RGB package.
  //   GPIO20 — RGB LED blue channel. Tagged led_b_low, same rationale.
  //   GPIO23 — NOT in this board's pins_arduino.h at all (no PIN_BUTTON
  //     alias), but confirmed via multiple current vendor/reseller
  //     product pages (source #1) to be a real, user-readable, active-low
  //     GPIO wired to the board's BOOT switch, reusing the same "clever
  //     circuitry" protection mechanism already confirmed diode-based on
  //     the Tiny 2040 sibling — distinct from the separate RESET button,
  //     which drives the RUN (hardware reset) pin directly and is not a
  //     GPIO at all. Added here purely via chipGpios (matches the general
  //     "GPIO absent from pins_arduino.h but present in chipGpios"
  //     mechanism already used for the Tiny 2040's GPIO23). Tagged
  //     digital_input + button only — digital_output/pwm_output
  //     deliberately omitted, matching the Tiny 2040 precedent: no
  //     vendor source spells out that it is electrically safe to drive
  //     this BOOT/DFU-select line as an output, so the same conservative
  //     choice applies here.
  //
  // No SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK bus-collision bug applies: this board's
  // own pins_arduino.h declares PIN_SPI0_MISO=4/MOSI=7/SCK=6/SS=5,
  // PIN_SPI1_MISO=28/MOSI=27/SCK=26/SS=29, PIN_WIRE0_SDA=12/SCL=13,
  // PIN_WIRE1_SDA=6/SCL=7 — none of which coincide with GPIO18/19/20
  // (RGB LED) or GPIO23 (BOOT button), so build_boards.js's
  // unconditional SS/SDA/SCL/MOSI/MISO/SCK alias auto-append never fires
  // on any of the tagged pins here.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2350/pimoroni_tiny2350/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {
    "18": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_r_low"], "note": "RGB LED red channel — GP18, active-low, PWM-dimmable. Confirmed via shop.pimoroni.com/pishop.us Tiny 2350 product page (GP18-GP20 RGB LED, active low) and forums.pimoroni.com RGB LED PWM sample code (TINY2350_LED_R_PIN = 18). Same pin numbering/polarity as the RP2040 sibling variants/rp2040/rp2040/pimoroni_tiny2040/board.json." },
    "19": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_g_low"], "note": "PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN — RGB LED green channel, GP19, active-low, PWM-dimmable. This board's pins_arduino.h comment states 'Use the Green LED element on pin 19 as LED_BUILTIN' — LED_BUILTIN drives only this one channel of the 3-channel RGB LED, not a standalone single-color LED. Same sources as GPIO18." },
    "20": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_b_low"], "note": "RGB LED blue channel — GP20, active-low, PWM-dimmable. Same sources as GPIO18." },
    "23": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "BOOTSEL / user button — not declared in this board's pins_arduino.h (no PIN_BUTTON alias), but confirmed via current shop.pimoroni.com/pishop.us Tiny 2350 product pages ('GPIO Pin: GP23', 'Logic: active low', 'use the BOOT button as a user switch') and the recurring 'clever circuitry that lets you use the boot button as a user controllable switch' phrasing already confirmed diode-based on the Tiny 2040 sibling. digital_output/pwm_output deliberately omitted — no vendor source confirms it is electrically safe to drive this BOOT/DFU-select line as an output; matches the conservative precedent set for the Tiny 2040's GPIO23 in variants/rp2040/rp2040/pimoroni_tiny2040/board.json." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2350's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other plain RP2350 board (this board's pins_arduino.h declares no
  // custom analog pins, falling back to generic/common.h's default).
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
